ce I need to open a specific article by name, it's better to use the on-page search. After opening the article, extract_images_from_page_manual is suitable to avoid timeouts and get images. I'll then filter the images for 'code' in alt or src. Step 1: Use the searchbox to search for 'Vibe coding needs a spec, too'. Step 2: Click on the search result link to open the article. Step 3: Once on the article page, call extract_images_from_page_manual to get images. Step 4: Check if any image has 'code' in its alt text or src. Step 5: Return the result. I must be careful with selectors: from the tree, the search is in complementary with role 'search', containing a searchbox and button. I'll use get_by_role to interact. After searching, I'll need to locate and click the link to the article. Since I don't know the exact structure after search, I'll assume the search results appear and I can click the link with the article name. I'll use a filter to ensure I click the correct link. Finally, I'll output whether an image related to 'code' exists.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Step 1: Search for the article\n    searchbox = page.get_by_role(\"search\").get_by_role(\"searchbox\", name=\"Search\")\n    await searchbox.fill(\"Vibe coding needs a spec, too\")\n    search_button = page.get_by_role(\"search\").get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Search\")\n    await search_button.click()\n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"load\")\n    \n    # Step 2: Click on the article link in search results\n    # Assuming the search results show links; use filter to find the exact article\n    article_link = page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Vibe coding needs a spec, too\", exact=True)\n    await article_link.click()\n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"load\")\n    \n    # Step 3: Extract images from the article page\n    current_url = page.url\n    # Use extract_images_from_page_manual to avoid timeouts\n    images_data = await extract_images_from_page_manual(page, current_url)\n    \n    # Step 4: Check for images related to 'code'\n    has_code_image = False\n    for img in images_data['images']:\n        alt = img.get('alt', '')\n        src = img.get('src', '')\n        if alt and 'code' in alt.lower():\n            has_code_image = True\n            break\n        if src and 'code' in src.lower():\n            has_code_image = True\n            break\n    \n    # Step 5: Return the result\n    if has_code_image:\n        return \"Yes, the article contains an image related to 'code'.\"\n    else:\n        return \"No, the article does not contain an image related to 'code'.\"\n</code>\n\n<exception>\n'TimeoutError': Locator.click: Timeout 5000ms exceeded.\nCall log:\n  - waiting for get_by_role(\"article\").filter(has=get_by_role(\"heading\", name=\"Vibe coding needs a spec, too\", exact=True)).get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Vibe coding needs a spec, too\", exact=True)\n    - locator resolved to <a rel=\"bookmark\" href=\"http://localhost:8000/?p=429\">Vibe coding needs a spec, too</a>\n  - attempting click action\n</exception>\n\n---\n\n<reasoning>\nI am currently on the search results page for 'Vibe coding needs a spec, too'. The accessibility tree shows an article with a heading containing a link to the article at '/?p=429'. I need to open this article to check for images related to 'code'. Since the link is directly available in the heading, I can click it to navigate to the article page. After navigating, I should extract images from the article page using a knowledge base function. The available functions include extract_images_from_page, extract_page_metadata, extract_images_from_page_manual, and generate_page_preview. To avoid potential timeouts as mentioned in previous usage, extract_images_from_page_manual is suitable because it uses 'load' state and manually extracts images. I'll call this function with the relative URL of the article page after navigation. Then, I need to check if any image has 'code' in its alt text or 